                <h2>This Week's Observation: Creative That 'Runs' vs. Creative That 'Sells'</h2>
<p>If you've been managing Meta ads recently, you've probably seen this pattern: a creative hits 3% CTR, maintains a healthy CPM, and runs smoothly through the learning phase. Everything looks great on the surface. Then you pull the conversion data, and the ROAS tells a completely different story.</p>
<p>This is the most common issue we observed this week: <strong>ads that 'run' but don't 'sell.'</strong></p>
<p>The root cause usually isn't account structure or bidding strategy—it's how we review creatives. Too many advertisers focus only on 'running' metrics and miss the signals that actually matter: whether users convert.</p>
<hr />
<h2>'Running' vs. 'Selling': Two Different Problems</h2>
<p>Let's clarify the distinction:</p>
<p><strong>'Running'</strong> means the creative passes Meta's algorithm cold start, earns sufficient impressions, and maintains a reasonable CPM. Key indicators:</p>
<ul>
<li>Initial CPM doesn't spike unexpectedly</li>
<li>Learning phase completes within 3-5 days</li>
<li>Impression volume meets target goals</li>
</ul>
<p><strong>'Selling'</strong> means the creative converts impressions into actions—purchases, sign-ups, or payments. Key indicators:</p>
<ul>
<li>Conversion rate exceeds category baseline</li>
<li>Landing page bounce rate stays manageable</li>
<li>Cost per acquisition (CPA) aligns with profitability model</li>
</ul>
<p><strong>The critical distinction</strong>: 'Running' is algorithmic approval. 'Selling' is user approval. These can overlap, but they don't guarantee each other.</p>
<hr />
<h2>A Practical Creative Review Checklist</h2>
<p>Here's a framework we compiled this week, applicable to e-commerce, apps, gaming, and other major verticals. Run through each section to quickly identify where the problem lies.</p>
<h3>Step 1: Check 'Selling' Metrics First</h3>
<table>
<thead>
<tr>
<th>Metric</th>
<th>'Selling' Standard</th>
<th>Warning Signs</th>
</tr>
</thead>
<tbody>
<tr>
<td>Conversion Rate</td>
<td>1.5x category baseline</td>
<td>High impressions, zero conversions</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>CPA</td>
<td>Below 1/3 of target LTV</td>
<td>Stable volume but rising costs</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>Landing Page Retention</td>
<td>30s retention &gt; 40%</td>
<td>Large gap between ad and landing page</td>
</tr>
</tbody>
</table>
<p>If 'selling' metrics underperform, don't immediately blame the account—check landing page experience and creative-to-product alignment first.</p>
<h3>Step 2: Then Check 'Running' Metrics</h3>
<table>
<thead>
<tr>
<th>Metric</th>
<th>'Running' Standard</th>
<th>Warning Signs</th>
</tr>
</thead>
<tbody>
<tr>
<td>Impressions</td>
<td>10k+ within 3 days (depending on scale needs)</td>
<td>Stuck in learning phase</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>CPM</td>
<td>Within 120% of category average</td>
<td>High CPM consuming budget</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>CTR</td>
<td>1%-3% as reference baseline</td>
<td>Abnormally high CTR but poor conversions</td>
</tr>
</tbody>
</table>
<p>One trap to watch for: <strong>abnormally high CTR</strong> (e.g., 5%+) paired with extremely low conversions often signals clickbait creatives. Users click, discover a mismatch, and leave immediately. These may 'run' short-term but damage account quality scores long-term.</p>
<h3>Step 3: Segment Creatives by Performance</h3>
<p>Classify your active creatives into three tiers:</p>
<ol>
<li><strong>Runs and sells</strong>: Scale up aggressively</li>
<li><strong>Runs but doesn't sell</strong>: Pause or reduce budget; optimize landing page first</li>
<li><strong>Doesn't run or sell</strong>: Turn off immediately</li>
</ol>
<hr />
<h2>A Real Case This Week</h2>
<p>A utility app team ran a creative that achieved 2.8% CTR—well above category average—but their 7-day paid conversion rate was only 0.3% against an expected 1.2%.</p>
<p>Investigation revealed the issue wasn't the ad at all. Their landing page was in English, but traffic was coming from Southeast Asia via TikTok. The page lacked localization, and the CTA button text even contained spelling errors.</p>
<p><strong>Lesson learned</strong>: Sometimes 'running' creatives are covering for landing page problems. Review the entire funnel, not just the ad metrics.</p>
<hr />
<h2>Recommendations for the Week</h2>
<ol>
<li>
<p><strong>Establish a 'selling' baseline before scaling</strong>: Confirm category conversion benchmarks, target CPA, and landing page performance before increasing spend. This gives you a real benchmark to judge whether creatives 'sell.'</p>
</li>
<li>
<p><strong>Review creatives at least weekly</strong>: Creative fatigue sets in fast. Weekly reviews catch decline signals early and allow timely replacements.</p>
</li>
<li>
<p><strong>Don't worship single metrics</strong>: High CTR doesn't equal great creative. Low CPM doesn't equal targeted audience. Context matters—evaluate everything together.</p>
</li>
</ol>
<p>That's our observation for this week. If you're facing 'runs but doesn't sell' challenges in your own reviews, stay tuned to Yiyu Media for more insights.</p>
